markdown <<MARKDOWN
<h1>What Is a Flowchart, and Why AI Needs to Read It</h1>

<p>Think about a social media app deciding what to show you after you like one video. Someone designed that rule. Apps are full of decision points like this, and each one shapes what a user sees or experiences next. Before students write a single conditional in code, this lesson asks them to notice that decisions like this exist everywhere, and that someone, eventually maybe them, is the one designing the rule.</p>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px;">
<p>📖 <b>Flowchart</b> is a diagram that shows the steps in a process using shapes and arrows. Flowcharts make it easier to plan and visualize how a program or process works.</p>
</div>

---

<h2>The Four Symbols</h2>

<p>Flowcharts use a small, standardized set of shapes, and each one has a specific job:</p>

<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 20px; margin: 20px 0;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/993b8dc6e46f2adb2472482c3487680f-image-1786465682635.png" alt="A diagram showing standard flowchart symbols connected by arrows: a rounded shape for Start or End, a rectangle for Process, a diamond for Decision, and connecting lines showing flow." style="width: 35%; max-width: 180px; border-radius: 10px; flex-shrink: 0;">
  <div style="flex: 1;">
    <p style="margin: 0 0 8px 0;">• <b>Start/End:</b> where the process begins or stops</p>
    <p style="margin: 0 0 8px 0;">• <b>Process:</b> an action the app or user takes</p>
    <p style="margin: 0 0 8px 0;">• <b>Decision:</b> a question with Yes/No outcomes, usually drawn as a diamond</p>
    <p style="margin: 0;">• <b>Connector:</b> shows the flow of logic from one step to the next</p>
  </div>
</div>

---

<h2>Why AI Needs to Read It Too</h2>

<p>AI uses computer vision to interpret flowcharts, recognizing shapes and reading text to translate a picture into working code.</p>

<div style="text-align: center; margin: 20px 0;">
<img src="https://images.code.org/e71c27521c0c8bfc21eca09b9e5e70a1-image-1786465740444.png" alt="A camera-based computer vision device scanning a printed diagram, representing how AI reads and interprets visual shapes and text." style="max-width: 400px; width: 100%; border-radius: 10px;">
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; color: #222222; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 20px 0;">
<p><strong>💡 Teacher tip:</strong> standardization matters here for two audiences at once. It's what makes a student's flowchart readable by a classmate or by you. But it's also what makes AI able to correctly interpret a photo of that flowchart and build a working program from it. A messy, non-standard flowchart isn't just harder for people to follow, it's harder for AI to translate accurately too.</p>
</div>

<p>Next, you'll see how one specific shape, the decision diamond, becomes a specific piece of code: the conditional statement.</p>


